592186045417 true] [17592186068684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545852 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545849 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545817 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545838 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545828 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545813 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186068843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545835 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069256 1071 "![](images/af/afcd7f6646b84debcea24ad6bf3a9cd71d14aae778cdb31b77a36665865a1996.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $8\\,\\text{cm}?$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069256 1071 "![](images/af/afcd7f6646b84debcea24ad6bf3a9cd71d14aae778cdb31b77a36665865a1996.png)\n\nWhat is the circumference of a circle with a radius of $8cm$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186069256 1074 "We use the formula $C=2\\pi r$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 8 \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069431 1071 "Ken's tree has a diameter of $4\\,\\text{ft}.$ What length of chain does Ken need for it to wrap around the tree perfectly?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186069431 1071 "Ken's tree has a diameter of $4ft$. What length of chain does Ken need for it to wrap around the tree perfectly?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186069431 1074 "The length of the chain needed to wrap around the tree is its circumference.\n\nThe formula for the circumference of a circle is $C=\\pi d,$ where $d=4\\,\\text{ft}$ is the diameter.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nC=\\pi \\cdot 4=4\\pi \\,\\text{ft}.\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186071780 1078 #edb/ref 17592186071783 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186073681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545819 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186073681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545858 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186073899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545821 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186074257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186074257 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\arctan (0.8)$ in radians?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186074257 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.8)$ in radians?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186074257 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0.8)=0.674\\,740…≈0.675\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186079757 1078 #edb/ref 17592186079762 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186079781 1078 #edb/ref 17592186079785 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186079787 1078 #edb/ref 17592186079791 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186079991 1078 #edb/ref 17592186079992 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186079997 1078 #edb/ref 17592186079998 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080004 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080005 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080006 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080007 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186080008 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545221 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545296 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545549 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545570 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545651 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080003 1078 #edb/ref 17592186545570 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186080021 1078 #edb/ref 17592186080025 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083028 1071 "![](images/ac/ac3704f44a4719bf20040a66410c3ed4c9251a9a2c6775204d61c537ac9eaf83.png)\n\nA country keeps a record of its population growth. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the population (in millions) and the time (in years) since records began.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?\n\n1. Linear\n2. Quadratic\n3. Exponential" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083028 1071 "![](images/ac/ac3704f44a4719bf20040a66410c3ed4c9251a9a2c6775204d61c537ac9eaf83.png)\n\nA country keeps a record of its population growth. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the population (in millions) and the time (in years) since records began.\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?\n\nI. Linear\n\nII. Quadratic\n\nIII. Exponential" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083139 1074 "![](images/b9/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083139 1074 "![](images/b9/b970e539af5b695644486ad4eb7c21558c334ffd33c1df1a2343fac493d84389.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, we have a *negative* correlation.\n- The points are scattered quite far from the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *weak* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *weak negative* linear correlation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083140 1071 "![](images/4e/4e3c58da0dc7f326497d9a7c1327d6d40f98684089694f9de88f21a64f4b4824.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?\n\n1. $\\rho =-0.8$\n2. $\\rho= 0.3$\n3. $\\rho = 0.9$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083140 1071 "![](images/4e/4e3c58da0dc7f326497d9a7c1327d6d40f98684089694f9de88f21a64f4b4824.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the correlation coefficient for the data in the scatter plot above?\n\nI. $\\rho =-0.8$\n\nII. $\\rho= 0.3$\n\nIII. $\\rho = 0.9$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083140 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/06/06dcc617bbbc07fea756f35975146a238bd4cb9c4ce437f1b9300a7c05ded7b6.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n$\\rho = -0.8$\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1$. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n$\\rho = 0.3$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient from the given options is $\\rho= 0.9$." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083140 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/06/06dcc617bbbc07fea756f35975146a238bd4cb9c4ce437f1b9300a7c05ded7b6.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *positive*. Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = -0.8\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho = 0.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient from the given options is $\\rho= 0.9.$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083141 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y$. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85$, sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083141 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85,$ sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083141 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\rho \\approx -0.85\n$$\n\nthere is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](images/ad/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083141 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\approx -0.85,$ there is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](images/ad/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545865 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545876 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545807 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545831 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545845 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083229 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545856 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545848 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545866 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083337 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545874 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545798 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545811 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545841 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545868 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545794 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545854 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545862 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545878 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083427 1050 420.0 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083429 1050 420.0 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083477 1071 "Which of the following scatter plots shows a possible quadratic dependence between $x$ and $y$?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083477 1071 "Which of the following scatter plots shows a possible quadratic dependence between $x$ and $y?$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083477 1074 "In the case of quadratic dependence, the points on the plot should be scattered along a parabola.\n\nAmong the given options, the only scatter plot that fits the description is the following:\n\n![](images/fa/fa4b708819d3ab941b7a037150333b743dad9e26610d1223c0ad5d306a449f17.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083481 1071 "![](images/89/89aef4df3a3de7030d53a2fb2d6382087e9b744dbffa203fed521a230a00c6ba.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y$. Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083481 1071 "![](images/89/89aef4df3a3de7030d53a2fb2d6382087e9b744dbffa203fed521a230a00c6ba.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a correspondence between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Which of the following is the most appropriate regression model for this data?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083481 1074 "It seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no correlation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083482 1074 "As values of $x$ increase, the corresponding values of $y$ first increase and then decrease. It seems like the points on the plot are scattered along a parabola, as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/20acefe1f03a1ba3dd7154eb0ec9ab462009ed1153d7edb282807fac57130328.png)\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the most appropriate regression model is *quadratic.*" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083516 1074 "![](images/6b/6b21527790fddecf89cd72a2e1d1f67338d21d82c1f3090536be973fcd60919a.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *positive* slope. So, we have a *positive* correlation.\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, we have a *strong* correlation.\n\nTherefore, the variables have a *strong positive* linear correlation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083517 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083518 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/91/91d605a297f2fd3c90c59592cc08184c4201f04ebc69bfc285e4261af8e918d2.png)\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The points in the scatter plot seem to follow a straight line with a *negative* slope. So, the correlation coefficient must be *negative*. Thus, we disregard the following values:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈0.9,\\,\\rho ≈0.1,\\,\\rho ≈0\n$$\n\n- The points lie pretty close to the corresponding trend line. So, the correlation coefficient must be closer to $-1.$ Thus, we disregard the following value:\n\n$$\n\\rho ≈-0.2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈-0.8.$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083521 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the number of years passed since $2000$ and the population of a particular town. Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho ≈0.9,$ which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186083521 1071 "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the number of years passed since $2000$ and the population of a particular town. Given that the correlation coefficient is $ρ \\approx 0.9$, which of the following scatter plots corresponds to this situation?" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186083521 1074 "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho ≈0.9,$ there is a *strong positive* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *positive* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *positive* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation corresponds to the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/aa/aac79b793bde599899ff7ff8ca880e198bd5481178c2c5a0e2ac96acf223bdaa.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186084452 1064 "There is a **linear correlation** between two sets of paired observations if the data points on the corresponding scatter plot are located along a straight line.\n\nWe say that the correlation is **positive** if the corresponding trend line has a *positive slope*. Similarly, we say that the correlation is **negative** if the corresponding trend line has a *negative slope*.\n\nWe have a **strong** correlation if the data points lie *close* to the corresponding trend line. Similarly, we have a **weak** correlation if the data points are scattered *far* from the corresponding trend line.\n\nLet's see a few examples:\n\n| ![](images/cd/cdd335fda9e91cbf7cdb05ad18065630985c50adb788df980708d822f7a5e8e9.png) | **Strong positive** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *positive* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | **Weak positive** linear correlation: \n\n- The trend line has a *positive* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | **Strong negative** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *negative* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| ![](images/07/076440b257a78474e9f1b3129ec448a407bb1b49215a29251d8e929cadaeb984.png) | **Weak negative** linear correlation:\n\n- The trend line has a *negative* slope.\n\n- The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/ed/ed266d09a86d7200c02f004cd9bb3a87f75693462ff8846f7f3cc1ed4b66ed63.png) | **No linear correlation**:\n\n- The data points are scattered randomly. |" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186084452 1064 "There is a **linear correlation** between two sets of paired observations if the data points on the corresponding scatter plot are located along a straight line.\n\nWe say that the correlation is **positive** if the corresponding trend line has a *positive slope*. Similarly, we say that the correlation is **negative** if the corresponding trend line has a *negative slope*.\n\nWe have a **strong** correlation if the data points lie *close* to the corresponding trend line. Similarly, we have a **weak** correlation if the data points are scattered *far* from the corresponding trend line.\n\nLet's see a few examples:\n\n| ![](images/cd/cdd335fda9e91cbf7cdb05ad18065630985c50adb788df980708d822f7a5e8e9.png) | **Strong positive** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *positive* slope. The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | **Weak positive** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *positive* slope. The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | **Strong negative** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *negative* slope. The data points lie *close* to the trend line. |\n| ![](images/07/076440b257a78474e9f1b3129ec448a407bb1b49215a29251d8e929cadaeb984.png) | **Weak negative** linear correlation:<br>The trend line has a *negative* slope. The data points lie *far* from the trend line. |\n| ![](images/ed/ed266d09a86d7200c02f004cd9bb3a87f75693462ff8846f7f3cc1ed4b66ed63.png) | **No linear correlation**:<br>The data points are scattered randomly. |" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186084490 1064 "The linear **correlation coefficient** $\\rho$ (the Greek letter rho, pronounced \"row\") is a real number between $-1$ and $1$ that shows the strength of a linear correlation. It has the following properties:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation\n\n- $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation\n\n- $\\rho = 0$ indicates no (linear) correlation\n\nThe approximate value of $\\rho$ can be determined from the scatter plot.\n\n| ![](images/09/09d585da995df4e5a67b022816eec246113171d3aad15dcb57d82c766dcb7d18.png) | For a strong positive correlation, we have $0.7 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1.$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | For a weak positive correlation, we have $0.3 \\leq \\rho \\lt 0.7.$ |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | For a strong negative correlation, we have $-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq -0.7.$ |\n| ![](images/92/92b65a115fd1621303a248de8ae03d05392827914f5200f267d368867d511ae5.png) | For a weak negative correlation, we have $-0.7 \\lt \\rho \\leq -0.3.$ |\n| ![](images/2c/2cf1e2c3e79940229fc85c10d5bccdfed17afe0759cc897d53e5421328b21466.png) | For little or no correlation, we have $-0.3 < \\rho < 0.3.$ |" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186084490 1064 "The linear **correlation coefficient** $\\rho$ (the Greek letter rho, pronounced \"row\") is a real number between $-1$ and $1$ that shows the strength of a linear correlation. It has the following properties:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation\n- $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation\n- $\\rho = 0$ indicates no (linear) correlation\n\nThe approximate value of $\\rho$ can be determined from the scatter plot.\n\n| ![](images/09/09d585da995df4e5a67b022816eec246113171d3aad15dcb57d82c766dcb7d18.png) | For a strong positive correlation, we have $0.7 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1$. |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](images/9b/9b288c25fb0993a714768e3e2e9caf23a11f2629950eb03b138acfc0aa6c17cf.png) | For a weak positive correlation, we have $0.3 \\leq \\rho \\lt 0.7$. |\n| ![](images/36/3680700a5e4e4e196db6ffe106535ad784567b9fbed85e5005cbb6e49a476860.png) | For a strong negative correlation, we have $-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq -0.7$. |\n| ![](images/92/92b65a115fd1621303a248de8ae03d05392827914f5200f267d368867d511ae5.png) | For a weak negative correlation, we have $-0.7 \\lt \\rho \\leq -0.3$. |\n| ![](images/2c/2cf1e2c3e79940229fc85c10d5bccdfed17afe0759cc897d53e5421328b21466.png) | For little