 of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n{r =-4\\cos θ; r = 2\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4\\cos θ &= 2 \\\\\n\\cos θ &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,360^\\circ)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\theta =120^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = 240^\\circ\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $B$ are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\big(2, \\, 240^\\circ\\big)\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186105614 1071 "Find the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r =3+3\\cos\\theta$ and $r = 4,$ shown below. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate.\n\n![](images/cb/cbdc7d93d48f21d3d4ae67a5cb7692801d240e896263a137557e67f8a5bfbcfc.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186105614 1071 "Find the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r =3+3\\cos\\theta$ and $r = 4$, shown below. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate.\n\n![](images/cb/cbdc7d93d48f21d3d4ae67a5cb7692801d240e896263a137557e67f8a5bfbcfc.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186105614 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n{r = 3 + 3\\cos θ; r = 4\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3+3\\cos \\theta & = 4 \\\\[5pt] 3\\cos \\theta & = 1 \\\\[3pt] \\cos \\theta & = \\dfrac 1 3. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 \\approx 70.5^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2 \\approx 289.5\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\nNotice that we must have\n$r=4$\nat any of the points of intersection.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(4, 70.5 \\, ^\\circ \\right)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad (r,\\theta)=\\left(4, 289.5 \\, ^\\circ\\right)\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186105614 1074 "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3+3\\cos \\theta & = 4\\[5pt]\n \t \t3\\cos \\theta & = 1   \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\cos \\theta & = \\dfrac 1 3.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ),$ we obtain $\\theta_1 \\approx 70.5^{\\circ}$ and $\\theta_2 \\approx 289.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\nNotice that we must have \n$r=4$\n at any of the points of intersection.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 70.5 \\, ^\\circ \\right)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad (r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 289.5 \\, ^\\circ\\right).\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186105615 1071 "The circles $r=4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=4\\sqrt{3}\\sin  \\theta$ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown in the diagram. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n![](images/27/2766090535f1931a0cb15a248eb62c9b1dc5b5f9bc15b6dff5d544876527d77c.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186105615 1071 "The circles $r=4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=4\\sqrt{3}\\sin \\theta$ intersect at points $O$ and $A$, as shown in the diagram. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A$?\n\n![](images/27/2766090535f1931a0cb15a248eb62c9b1dc5b5f9bc15b6dff5d544876527d77c.png)" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186105615 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nis an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt{3}\\sin θ &= 4\\cos θ \\\\\n\\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} &= \\frac{4}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nin the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation\n\n$$\nr=4\\cos \\theta\n$$\n\nto find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186105615 1074 "First of all, we notice from the graph that $(r,\\theta)=\\left(0, 0\\right)$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{4}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4\\cos \\theta$ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(r,\\theta)=\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right).$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186105760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545795 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186105760 1038 #edb/ref 17592186545804 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [1759218