 our solid can be written as a type III region: \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le z\\le 0,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 5-3{z}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{5-3{z}^{2}}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}[y]_{y=0}^{y=5-3{z}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}5-3{z}^{2}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[5z-{z}^{3}]_{z=-1}^{z=0}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0-(5(-1)-(-1)^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5-1\\,dx \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}dx \\\\  & =4[x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186281316 1074 "![](images/8e/8e5f3095cac4e068e54d9b3ac2bb55dc4789e7ca68d4524dce4d25a55abd5be6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(x),$ we substitute $y=0$  into the equation $y=4-2x-4z:$\n\n$$\n0=4-2x-4z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76fcca4251a904a08ac2f6ba8181d92c2c2fa7e07e596ef6276f1d21b574817a.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x-4z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-2x-4z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}(4-2x-4z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}4-2x-4z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(4-2x)z-2{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1-x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((4-2x)(1-\\frac{x}{2})-2{(1-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2})((4-2x)-2(1-\\frac{x}{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)(2-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2(2)-{2}^{2}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186281316 1074 "![](images/8e/8e5f3095cac4e068e54d9b3ac2bb55dc4789e7ca68d4524dce4d25a55abd5be6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nTo find the top boundary curve of $D$ in the form $z=z(x),$ we substitute $y=0$  into the equation $y=4-2x-4z:$\n\n$$\n0=4-2x-4z\\,⟹\\,z=1-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/76/76fcca4251a904a08ac2f6ba8181d92c2c2fa7e07e596ef6276f1d21b574817a.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-\\frac{x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-2x-4z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2x-4z}dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-2x-4z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}(4-2x-4z)-0\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x/2}4-2x-4z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[(4-2x)z-2{z}^{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=1-x/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}((4-2x)(1-\\frac{x}{2})-2{(1-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2})-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2})((4-2x)-2(1-\\frac{x}{2}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)(2-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2-2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2x-{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2(2)-{2}^{2}+\\frac{{2}^{3}}{6})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186320339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186545276 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186320339 1078 #edb/ref 17592186319850 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186320339 1078 #edb/ref 17592186545276 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186396387 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186396387 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}-(Δx)^{4}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.3,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.1,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.3$ to $Δx=0.1,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139535941 17592186045417 false] [17592186545183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545888 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186545183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545962 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186545183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186545966 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186545183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186546010 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [17592186545183 1043 #edb/ref 17592186546018 13194139535941 17592186045417 true] [1759218654